@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+schema_version: "1.0"
+name: compose-a-behavioural-graph
+description: >-
+  A program with unit tests and no behavioural validation. Does a test_graph
+  project come out of it -- a registered graph with a node it actually refers
+  to -- or another unit test in a new directory?
+runs: 1
+execution:
+  env:
+    EVAL_CASE: compose-a-behavioural-graph
+  # Bash IS granted here: scaffolding is what the skill's scripts do, and a
+  # case that forbids the shell would measure whether the agent can retype a
+  # scaffold by hand. `evals/run.sh` derives the grant from this line.
+  max_turns: 60
+  timeout_seconds: 1800
+  allowed_tools: [Read, Glob, Grep, Skill, Bash, Write, Edit]
+  prompt: |
+    This repository has unit tests and no behavioural validation: nothing here
+    exercises the program the way a user would.
+
+    Set up validation for it and compose one graph that covers the behaviour
+    the unit tests cannot: a slug claimed by one owner, released, and claimed
+    again by a different one.
+
+    End your reply by naming the graph you registered and the node it runs.
+    The grader reads your reply and cannot open the files you wrote.
diff --git a/evals/test-graph/compose-a-behavioural-graph/graders/a-graph-with-a-node-in-it.md b/evals/test-graph/compose-a-behavioural-graph/graders/a-graph-with-a-node-in-it.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..bd150007
--- /dev/null
+++ b/evals/test-graph/compose-a-behavioural-graph/graders/a-graph-with-a-node-in-it.md
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+---
+type: file_exists
+path: ".eval/graph"
+weight: 2
+---
+
+Written by the `Stop` hook only after `checks/testgraph_scaffold.py` finds a
+`test_graph/` project with a settings file, a build file that registers a graph
+by name, at least one node file that describes itself, and a reference from the
+registered graph to that node.
+
+The last clause is the one that matters. A scaffold with an empty graph beside
+an orphan node file is the cheap shape, and it satisfies every check except
+"do they refer to each other".
+
+**A red here is not always the work's, and this suite cannot say which.**
+Running the graph would be better evidence and is deliberately not attempted:
+the node runtime wants Gradle and a JBang or uv toolchain the eval sandbox does
+not reliably carry, and a check that cannot run leaves the same absent path as
+work that was never done. What was missing is printed into `.eval/verify.log`,
+which is where to look before reading a 0 as the agent's.
+
+The agent cannot write this path: `verify.sh` clears `.eval/` first, and the
+check runs write-denied.
diff --git a/evals/test-graph/compose-a-behavioural-graph/graders/names-the-graph-and-the-node.md b/evals/test-graph/compose-a-behavioural-graph/graders/names-the-graph-and-the-node.md
